course: arts
lesson: 37
title: Being bad at something and keeping going
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Everyone is bad at a new skill at first: guitar strings hurt, paintings go muddy, the hands you draw look like bunches of bananas, and that is the normal start, not a sign you were "not made for it".
explain: Skills grow by practice, but the clever kind: find the one hard bit, do it slowly and correctly a few times, then put it back, instead of playing the whole piece and making the same mistake every day.
explain: Online you see other people's best take after years of practice and lots of tries, so compare yourself with yourself last month, not with a stranger's highlight.
explain: Rest counts too: sleep helps your brain lock in a new movement, so a dance spin that failed tonight often works better tomorrow.
explain: People say it their own way: "nesh" in Yorkshire means soft or easily hurt, "peng" in London means brilliant, "butt" in Wales means mate, "lush" means lovely, and "the GOAT" means the greatest of all time.
fact: Guitar strings hurt beginners' fingertips; after a few weeks of short regular practice, the skin toughens into calluses, patches of hardened skin, and the pain fades.
fact: Videos online usually show someone's best take after much practice, often filmed many times and edited.
fact: The "10,000 hours" idea came from a popular book; the researcher behind it, Anders Ericsson, said there is no magic number and the kind of practice matters as much as the hours.
fact: Playing a whole piece over and over repeats the same mistake; musicians stop at the tricky bar (a small section of the music), play it slowly and correctly, then join it back in.
fact: Professional performers make mistakes on stage too; the skill is to keep going, and a small slip is often not noticed if the music does not stop.
fact: Mixing too many colours, or painting wet on wet, turns paint brown or grey, called muddy colour; using fewer colours and letting layers dry fixes it.
fact: It is often reported that Michael Jordan did not make his school's top basketball team at 15 and practised harder after that; the story is sometimes exaggerated.
fact: Research shows sleep helps the brain store new movement skills, so practice plus rest works better than practising tired for hours.
fact: Switching to a new hobby after giving the old one a fair try is a sensible choice, and skills like rhythm carry over.
fact: Hands are one of the hardest things to draw; drawing your own hand often, starting with simple shapes, helps.
fact: People start with different strengths, but skills in art, music and sport grow mainly through practice, and calling someone "born talented" can make them stop trying.
exercise: Ollie's fingertips are sore after one week of guitar. What is true? | Sore fingers are normal and toughen up with practice | His hands are not made for the guitar | He should practise five hours in one go | The strings are broken
exercise: What is a callus? | A patch of hardened skin | A part of the guitar that you press the strings on | A short song played by beginners in their first lesson | A cream that makes sore fingers stop hurting at once
exercise: Aisha watches a girl play piano perfectly on YouTube and wants to quit. What is fairer? | Compare herself with herself last month | Give up, because the girl was clearly born able to play perfectly | Practise until she is better than the girl by next week | Watch worse players only
exercise: Why are online videos unfair to compare yourself with? | They show a best take | They are always faked by computers, so the players cannot really play | People online are always much older than they say they are | Videos make every instrument sound easier than it really is
exercise: Daniel says you need ten thousand hours to be good at anything. What did the researcher say? | There is no magic number; how you practise matters | Exactly ten thousand hours, for every skill | You need twenty thousand hours if you start late | Talent is all that counts
exercise: Yuki makes the same mistake at bar 12 every day. What should she do? | Play the hard bit slowly and correctly, then put it back | Play the whole piece faster every time so it flows | Skip bar 12 in the concert and hope nobody hears | Play it all twice daily
exercise: What is a bar in music? | A small section of a piece | A long metal rod that holds up the piano lid | The sign at the start that tells you how loud to play | A kind of drum
exercise: Kyle drops his drumstick in the concert and freezes. What is the skill to learn? | Keep going after a slip | Stop the whole concert and start again from the very beginning | Run off the stage so that nobody can see him being embarrassed | Never play again
exercise: Valeria's sunset turns muddy brown. Why? | Too many colours were mixed or painted wet on wet | Her paints were old and had gone bad | Sunsets are always brown, so it is correct | She painted too slowly
exercise: What should Valeria do with her muddy painting? | Keep it and find the one thing to fix | Bin it, as it proves she is the worst painter in the world | Paint over it in black so that nobody ever sees it again | Say a friend painted it
exercise: Oleksii hears that Michael Jordan was cut from his team. What is the lesson? | A no today is not a no forever | Great players never fail at anything when they are young | The story proves that coaches never know anything about sport | Being cut from a team means you should quit the sport
exercise: Ama's auntie says she will "sleep on" the spin. Is that silly? | No, sleep helps the brain store new movements | Yes, sleeping cannot teach you dancing | Yes, she must practise all night instead | No, dreams show steps
exercise: Rory wants to switch from violin to drums. His mam says "you're not a quitter, butt". Which is fair? | Switching after a fair try is sensible | Changing hobby always means you are a quitter for life | He must play the violin until he is grown up, whatever happens | Drums are cheating
exercise: Ling hides every hand in a pocket. What would help her improve? | Drawing hands often, from simple shapes | Keep hiding hands in every picture for the rest of her life | Only drawing people who are wearing big gloves or mittens | Trace hands secretly
exercise: Nikhil's uncle says he is "born talented, no need to practise". What is true? | Practice is what moves skills forward | Talented people never need to practise anything at all | Nikhil will now be good at every kind of art without trying | Practice kills talent
exercise: Which practice usually works better? | Short practice, then rest | Practising for hours when very tired, without any breaks | Practising only once a month for a whole long day | Only watching videos of other people practising
test: Your mate goes, "My fingers hurt after a week, I'm nesh, guitar's not for me" ("nesh" means soft). What is the best answer? | Everyone's fingers hurt at first; they toughen up | He is right, some fingers always hurt on strings | He should use sharper strings to toughen up | He presses wrong strings
test: What would happen if Yuki kept playing the whole piece every day with the same mistake? | The mistake would stick | The mistake would fix itself after about a hundred tries | The piece would get easier without her doing anything else | Her teacher would remove bar 12 from the music for her
test: A London kid says, "She's peng at piano, I'm trash, why even try" ("peng" means brilliant). What should he remember? | He is seeing her best take after years | She was born playing piano perfectly with no practice | People who are trash at first are trash for ever | Only famous people can learn
test: In a cartoon, a boy plays drums perfectly after one lesson and becomes a rock star by Friday. What is more realistic? | Skills take many weeks of practice and mistakes | Everyone becomes great after their first lesson | Rock stars never practise, they just get lucky | Drums are the only instrument you can learn in a day
test: Your Gen Z cousin says the Jordan story means "the GOAT was mid as a kid" ("mid" means average). Which is fairer? | It is reported he was not picked at 15 and then practised hard | He was the best player in the world at the age of five | The story proves that he was never any good at basketball | He gave up basketball and became famous at something else
test: Kyle's brother says "you were wicked good after" ("wicked" means very). Why did nobody notice the dropped stick? | He kept playing and the music did not stop | Everyone in the hall had their eyes shut | The stick was invisible under the lights | Slips are always seen
test: A kid says, "Sleep is wasted time, I'll practise the dance till midnight." What is true? | Sleep helps lock in new skills | Practising tired for hours always works best for dancers | Sleep makes you forget everything you learned that day | Rest is only for grown-ups, not for children learning
test: Rory says, "I'm not quitting, I'm switching, that's lush" ("lush" means lovely). When is switching sensible? | After a fair try, choosing with your head | Every time a lesson feels hard or a bit boring | Only if your best friend switches to the same hobby | Never, finish everything
test: Which is the best plan for Ling, who cannot draw hands? | Ten quick hand sketches a week | Only ever drawing animals so hands never appear | Waiting until she grows up, when hands get easier | Copying hands exactly from another artist's comic
test: Valeria says her painting is "so mid" ("mid" means average) and wants to bin it. Which is wise? | Keep it, spot what went muddy, try again | Bin it and never paint a sunset ever again | Paint over it in black and pretend it is night | Blame the paints
test: Nikhil's teacher shakes her head at "born talent". Why? | Even talented people improve mainly by practising | Teachers do not believe that anyone has any talent | Talent is something that only adults can have | Nikhil cheated to win the drawing prize
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a beginner's fingertips cease to hurt after several weeks of guitar practice. | The skin toughens into calluses | The strings are replaced with softer strings by the teacher | The fingers lose all feeling permanently after practice | The guitar becomes smaller as it is played more often
exam: Which of the following most accurately characterises the view of Anders Ericsson concerning the "10,000 hours" rule? | No magic number exists, and the quality of practice matters | Precisely ten thousand hours are required for any skill whatsoever | Hours of practice are irrelevant in comparison with inborn talent | It applies only to music
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the effect of sleep upon the learning of movement skills. | Sleep assists the brain in storing new movement skills | Sleep causes the loss of movements practised the previous day | Sleep has no effect whatsoever upon any form of learning | Sleep assists learning solely in the case of adults
exam: Ascertain the method of practice most appropriate for correcting an error that recurs at one place in a piece of music. | Slow, correct repetition of that passage, then rejoining it | Repeated performance of the entire piece at increased speed | Omission of the difficult passage in all future performances | Abandonment of the piece in favour of a different one
